  "generated_text": "First, find the squares of each integer \\( n \\) from 1 to 10:\n\n\\[\n1^2 = 1, \\quad 2^2 = 4, \\quad 3^2 = 9, \\quad 4^2 = 16, \\quad 5^2 = 25, \\quad 6^2 = 36, \\quad 7^2 = 49, \\quad 8^2 = 64, \\quad 9^2 = 81, \\quad 10^2 = 100\n\\]\n\nNext, find the remainders when these squares are divided by 11:\n\n\\[\n1, 4, 9, 5, 3, 3, 5, 9, 4, 1\n\\]\n\nNow, sum the distinct remainders:\n\n\\[\n1 + 4 + 9 + 5 + 3 + 3 + 5 + 9 + 4 + 1 = 44\n\\]\n\nFinally, divide 44 by 11:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[4, '4']",
    "gold_answer": "First notice that $(11-n)^2=11^2-2\\cdot 11+n^2\\equiv n^2\\pmod{11}$, and since we're asked to find distinct results, we only need to compute the squares of $n=1,2,3,4,5$. Respectively, $n^2\\equiv 1,4,9,5,3\\pmod{11}$. Thus, $1+4+9+5+3=22=11\\cdot\\boxed{2}$.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
